text:
Looking at the board: I'm in great position with F MAO set to take POR, A SPA holding that center, and A BUR threatening BEL. I need to confirm with England whether they'll support me into BEL or contest it, and reassure Germany enough that they don't pivot west. Italy is irrelevant this turn. I should also check in with England about their plans for the fall — do they want to coordinate against Germany?

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Fall 1901 — I'm planning to push A BUR into BEL this turn to grab that supply center. I'd love your support from ENG or NTH if you're willing; alternatively, if you're also moving on BEL, let's coordinate so we don't bounce each other. What are your fall plans?",
  "GERMANY": "I held off Burgundy as agreed and I'm focusing on Iberia this fall — taking Portugal with my fleet. I hope you're doing the same going north and east rather than west. Let's keep the peace between us heading into 1902 and focus on where the real growth is."
}

Greece is contested between Austria and Turkey, and both want my backing. I'm committed to Tunis with F ION, so I can't influence Greece this turn anyway. I'll keep Austria warm (my Lepanto partner) and stay non-committal with Turkey while securing my own gain. No need to antagonize either over a center I'm not taking.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "Confirmed for this fall: F ION takes Tunis, A VEN holds, A APU stays home — your Trieste and Tyrolia stay untouched. Take Greece and Serbia for your three builds. With Turkey also eyeing Greece via the AEG side, you may want F ALB - GRE backed firmly. In Winter I build at Naples and we line up the Lepanto against Turkey in 1902.",
  "TURKEY": "I'm honoring my word — going to Tunis, nothing in the eastern Med. Greece is a real fight: Austria's F ALB is going for it too, so if you push GRE expect a bounce unless you commit force. I'm not contesting it myself either way. Lock down Bulgaria and the Black Sea first; that's your guaranteed gain this fall."
}

I have a clear picture heading into Fall 1901. England wants BEL and so do I — the key question is whether England will support me in or race me. Germany has promised to stay out of BEL and take HOL/DEN. I should commit firmly to my plan: A BUR → BEL (supported by F ENG if England agrees), F MAO → POR, A SPA holds or supports. England supporting me into BEL is the cleanest outcome — they get NWY, I get BEL, we both grow. I should make a final, concrete commitment to England and confirm with Germany.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Final commitment: I'm moving A BUR → BEL this turn. If you support me with F ENG → BEL, I get it cleanly and owe you a concrete favor in 1902 — specifically, I'll support your push into whatever German center you target. If you race me instead, we both bounce and neither gains. I'm trusting you on this; please confirm you'll support rather than contest.",
  "GERMANY": "Final confirmation: I'm moving A BUR → BEL this fall. I'm leaving HOL and DEN entirely to you, and I'm not touching MUN or RUH. Hold to our understanding and we both come out of 1901 ahead — I just need you to confirm no German unit is moving toward BEL."
}

My plan is set: take Tunis with F ION, keep A VEN home per my Austria pact, and keep A APU home as a future ferry/Venice support. Austria wants my support into Greece from ION, but my ION needs to go to Tunis for my Winter build — that's non-negotiable for my growth. I'll confirm with Austria that I can't support GRE this turn but I'm honoring the border, and stay quiet to Turkey since contesting Greece doesn't help me.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "Final word: F ION goes to Tunis (my Winter fleet build), so I can't support F ALB into GRE this turn — but your bounce with F ALB alone should still deny Turkey. A VEN holds, A APU holds; Trieste and Tyrolia untouched as promised. Take GRE and BUL, build your three, and we run the Lepanto against Turkey in 1902 with my new fleets.",
  "TURKEY": "As promised, I'm taking Tunis and staying out of the eastern Med entirely. Greece will be a hard fight against Austria's F ALB — your call whether to commit, but Bulgaria and Black Sea are your clean gains. Let's revisit Austria together in 1902 if they grow too fast."
}

Looking at the board: I have 7 centers and need to keep growing. England is angling for HOL and trying to steer me away from it — but HOL is adjacent to BEL and I can take it this turn. The key question is whether to push A BEL → HOL (risking a bounce with England's F NTH) or do something else with BEL. Given England's repeated messaging about wanting HOL, there's a real risk of collision. However, HOL is a free center and I need it. I'll push A BEL → HOL regardless — even a bounce costs me nothing while denying England. A MUN should press toward BER or KIE. Let me finalize the messaging: commit to England on the split deal while actually taking HOL myself; reassure Italy to keep them pressing Austria; and ignore Germany's pleas.

MESSAGES:
{
  "ENGLAND": "Final commitment: A BEL moves to HOL this turn — if your F NTH supports rather than contests, we guarantee it for France and you take KIE or DEN cleanly from the sea. If we bounce, Germany benefits and we both waste the turn. I won't touch KIE or DEN; take one of those and we finish Germany together this year.",
  "ITALY": "Committing: A MUN threatens BER or KIE this spring while A BEL takes HOL — Germany is finished this year. Push hard on TRI now; Austria at 6 centers with RUM is the biggest threat to both of us, and this is the turn to hit them before they consolidate further east."
}

This is the final round. France is at 8 and the runaway leader. Austria has repeatedly failed to land MUN — this Fall they claim A BOH→MUN with A TYR support, strength 2. France's A MUN holds (strength 1 if unsupported), so Austria's strike could actually land if France doesn't reinforce. But my four units can't crack MAR alone. My priority: clear ROM for a Winter build and consolidate. I've promised France a quiet border, which keeps me safe while I build up. But if I want growth, France collapsing east helps me long-term. Realistically, this turn I take NAP to clear ROM, hold my fleets, keep PIE forward. I'll keep France comfortable (honoring the deal nets me trust and a free build) while encouraging Austria to actually land MUN.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "I'm clearing ROM for a build this turn and keeping A PIE forward in the Alps to nominally fix France's MAR. If you genuinely land A BOH-MUN with A TYR support, France drops to 7 and the window finally opens — and next spring my built fleet plus PIE hit MAR for real. Land MUN this turn; that's the whole deal, and I'll commit hard once you hold it.",
  "FRANCE": "Border stays silent exactly as agreed — A PIE holds the Alps, no fleet enters the Gulf, I'm only clearing ROM for a Winter build. Be aware Austria is committing A BOH-MUN with A TYR support this Fall; if you want MUN held, garrison it. I'm not part of that — just keeping our deal clean."
}

I'll send final commitment messages to Germany and France, and stay silent with others since the key moves are already decided.

MESSAGES:
{
  "FRANCE": "Final commitment: F MAO - BRE and F NTH - BEL both proceed this spring with support — I have the strength to take both simultaneously. Your only path to saving one is to concede the other; if you order A BEL to hold and leave BRE undefended, I'll take BRE and hold off BEL next fall as promised.",
  "GERMANY": "Committing: F NTH - BEL this spring, F KIE holds steady and does not move toward BER. If you order A BER S F NTH - BEL, we crack France together and I'll hold KIE in place through Fall 1907 as a buffer against eastern pressure — Austria in MUN is the threat to you, not me."
}

Looking at the board: France messaged me about HOL last round, Germany is trying for MUN again, and I've already committed in my strategy notes to attacking BEL with F NTH + F HOL support at strength 2. France's message about HOL is irrelevant now since A BEL is still in BEL (France didn't take HOL in spring). I need to finalize my attack plan and get Germany's commitment on supporting BEL if possible, while warning France that BEL falls this turn.

MESSAGES:
{
  "FRANCE": "This fall F NTH moves to BEL supported by F HOL at strength 2 — A BEL cannot hold against that combination regardless of what you order. Your only real choice is where to put A BEL when it retreats; concede BEL now and I hold F MAO off BRE through Spring 1909 so you can stabilize against Turkey.",
  "GERMANY": "A BER supporting F NTH into BEL costs you nothing and guarantees it succeeds at strength 3 — Austria can't cut that support from MUN. After BEL falls this turn, I'll actively back your push into MUN with F KIE supporting from the flank; that's a concrete deal you can count on."
}
